From: Copy<br />
Subject: Failure to approve expenses</p>
<p>Hi there,</p>
<p>I understand that this time of year, as we work our way towards the holidays, is busy for everyone, so I hate to make extra work for you. But I just wanted to check in on what I believe is an error on the accounting department’s part.</p>
<p>You see, I recently submitted some expenses but was denied reimbursement, despite the fact that the expenses were crucial to my work as a copywriter.</p>
<p>To provide some more detail. On Tuesday, November 17th, we sold a two-pack of Star Patrol Rechargeable Flashlights. As part of my research, I watched the following video:</p>
<p><video controls src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/vse-vms-transcoding-artifact-us-east-1-prod/d4dea9ee-e527-4a39-a2c4-3297d881921b/default.jobtemplate.mp4.480.mp4"></video></p>
<p>At the 11-second mark, the video makes the claim that the Star Patrol can be seen from an airplane.</p>
<p>Now, unlike a lot of sites, Meh prides itself on its honesty. We don’t make sales-y hyperbolic claims like so many retailers. In fact, we seek to give our customers the whole truth.</p>
<p>Which is why, as the copywriter, I needed to know for certain whether or not this claim, regarding its visibility from an airplane, was true. Hence, I booked a round-trip flight to Miami, Florida. Also, let the record show that I bundled both the rental car and the four-night stay with the flight to save the company money.</p>
<p>Now, did I confirm the Star Patrol’s claim? Not exactly. My buddy Jim was supposed to shine one up at me, but he got mixed up about the flight routes. Still, the fact that I had recruited an unpaid volunteer to assist with the project shows further frugality on my part.</p>
<p>With this explanation provided, please approve the reimbursement for the flight.</p>
<p>Also, I’m providing an additional receipt for your review that I forgot to attach last time. It’s for 30,000 candles. Please reimburse this expense as well.</p>
<p>Best,<br />
Sean</p>